                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 7,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "nit: remove needless space"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 7,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Harshit Aghera",
                        "email": "haghera@nvidia.com",
                        "username": "harshit-aghera"
                    },
                    "message": "Done"
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Sensor/Type.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 5,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Deepak Kodihalli",
                        "email": "deepak.kodihalli.83@gmail.com",
                        "username": "dkodihal"
                    },
                    "message": "nit: I think this is the other way around. Objects implementing Sensor.Value may also implement Sensor.Type."
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Sensor/Type.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 5,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Yes.  Sensor.Type _requires_ Sensor.Value.  Sensor.Value _may_ implement Sensor.Type.\n\nDon't use non-standard directives like \"ideally\".  Follow ISO standards for requirements (copied from Redfish spec for your reference):\n\n> The terms \"shall\" (\"required\"), \"shall not\", \"should\" (\"recommended\"), \"should not\" (\"not recommended\"), \"may\", \"need not\" (\"not required\"), \"can\" and \"cannot\" in this document are to be interpreted as described in ISO/IEC Directives, Part 2, Clause 7."
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Sensor/Type.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 5,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Harshit Aghera",
                        "email": "haghera@nvidia.com",
                        "username": "harshit-aghera"
                    },
                    "message": "Done."
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Sensor/Type.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 27,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "I don't understand why we want to implement this on the BMC.  Can you add some use cases?  All daemons today expect \"Zero\" type sensors.  If we start adding other type sensors, daemons consuming sensors now need to be aware and be able to handle them.  This sounds like needless complexity."
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Sensor/Type.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 27,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Harshit Aghera",
                        "email": "haghera@nvidia.com",
                        "username": "harshit-aghera"
                    },
                    "message": "This DMTF property ReadingBasis can be used to indicate that the sensor value is not zero-based. \n\nExample -\nNvidia datacenter GPUs has a temperature sensor for the Thermal Limit (TLIMIT) temperature. It is a countdown in degrees Celsius to the threshold at which the closest software slowdown mechanism will engage. The reported TLIMIT temperature can be greater than 0 degrees Celsius if the current temperature is beyond a software slowdown threshold. The TLIMIT value can be a negative value during the period when a slowdown is engaged. The DMTF property ReadingBasis can be set to Headroom for this sensor."
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Sensor/Type.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 27,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Deepak Kodihalli",
                        "email": "deepak.kodihalli.83@gmail.com",
                        "username": "dkodihal"
                    },
                    "message": "@haghera@nvidia.com let's add this usecase to the commit message."
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Sensor/Type.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 27,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Harshit Aghera",
                        "email": "haghera@nvidia.com",
                        "username": "harshit-aghera"
                    },
                    "message": "Done."
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Sensor/Type.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 49,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Please keep these in alphabetical order or \"intuitively used\" order.\n\nUnknown, Zero, Delta, Headroom"
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Sensor/Type.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 49,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Harshit Aghera",
                        "email": "haghera@nvidia.com",
                        "username": "harshit-aghera"
                    },
                    "message": "Done."
                }
